Portable integer fixed point path
---------------------------------

The integer codec used to assume three things that C does not
guarantee: that "long" is exactly 32 bits, that left shifting a
negative signed integer keeps its sign, and that right shifting a
negative signed integer fills with the sign bit.  Built with the
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer the codec aborted on ordinary silence and
signed 16 bit PCM, first in the LAR reconstruction and then in the
preprocessing, LPC and RPE stages.

The arithmetic is now expressed with explicit, well defined rules.
inc/portable.h introduces exact-width internal types (16 bit word,
32 bit longword) and a small set of primitives that the rest of the
codec calls:

  - gsm_word_asl / gsm_longword_asl keep the low 16/32 bits of the
    two's complement word.  These are deliberate low-bit truncations,
    done on the matching unsigned type, not saturations.  The public
    gsm_asl / gsm_L_asl wrappers keep their historical edge behaviour
    for out-of-range and negative shift counts (count >= width gives
    0; count <= -width gives -1 for a negative input, 0 otherwise;
    a negative count reverses the shift direction).
  - gsm_word_asr / gsm_longword_asr are arithmetic right shifts that
    round towards negative infinity.  They are derived from the
    unsigned magnitude (floor division by a power of two); C division
    of negative values, which truncates towards zero, is never used to
    implement them.
  - The Q15 multiplies form the 16x16 product in the guaranteed 32 bit
    type and only then apply the floor shift, with the original
    round-to-nearest bias and the MIN_WORD*MIN_WORD saturation rule
    unchanged.  GSM_L_MULT keeps its 32 bit low-bit truncation.
  - gsm_norm works on an unsigned magnitude after flipping the bits of
    a negative input, so it no longer depends on a signed shift.

Saturating add/subtract (gsm_add, gsm_sub, gsm_L_add, gsm_L_sub,
gsm_abs) keep the existing GSM saturation rules; only the representation
of the intermediate magnitude changed.  The scaling and rounding
positions in the multiply-accumulate, LAR decode, normalized
correlation quantities and RPE inverse quantization were not moved.
The FAST/USE_FLOAT_MUL and LTP_CUT approximation branches and the
WAV49 byte packing were left structurally alone; the packing already
operates on unsigned quantities.

The supported target is an 8 bit byte, 16 bit short, 32 bit int, two's
complement machine with 32 or 64 bit long.  portable.h compile-time
checks these widths.  The header compiles both as C99 (the UBSan
build) and as strict ANSI C89 (the legacy Makefile).

Compatibility basis.  No algorithmic constant, table, scaling point or
rounding point changed, so every output bit is preserved: the normal
build still prints the fixed 33 byte frame and PCM fingerprints
(encoded=1727e7c8, pcm=7fc5a90a, nonzero=1918 over the 12 frame
devcheck sequence), and those values are identical at -O0 and -O2 and
whether or not SASR is defined.  The public gsm.h interface, the
gsm_signal type, the opaque gsm_state and the 33 byte frame layout are
unchanged, so existing callers and stored frame files stay compatible.

These fingerprints are only a regression tripwire for that one
sequence; they do not by themselves cover every fixed point boundary.
tests/portable_fixed.c separately pins the shift/rounding/saturation
edges (negative odd shifts, word-width edges, the minimum negative,
rounding and saturation boundaries), continuous silence/extrema/
impulse/noise streams with two differently primed decoder instances,
and independently constructed legal edge frames (edge LAR, RPE pulses,
all four grids and long term lag reuse) checked against the actual
decoded samples and their successor state, including two short cases
that change PCM if a scaling or rounding point is moved.  The expected
arithmetic values come from independent int64 quotient/remainder and
modular-truncation rules, never from the helpers being tested.

Run the full matrix (four build combinations under full UBSan plus the
original non-sanitized baseline) with:

	bash devcheck/target.sh

Minimal C usage, compiler and standard library only:

	#include "gsm.h"

	void frame_example(gsm enc, gsm dec,
			   gsm_signal in[160], gsm_signal out[160])
	{
		gsm_frame frame;

		gsm_encode(enc, in, frame);   /* 160 samples -> 33 bytes */
		if (gsm_decode(dec, frame, out) != 0)
			return;                 /* bad magic nibble        */
	}

For WAV #49 packing, enable the option on both the encoder and the
decoder before the first frame and feed the alternating 32/33 byte
packets in order; the half-byte chain and frame index are kept inside
the opaque state:

	int on = 1;
	gsm_option(enc, GSM_OPT_WAV49, &on);
	gsm_option(dec, GSM_OPT_WAV49, &on);
